WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:16.041
The steamer bound for America was called the General Grant and would take 12 days to cross the Pacific.

2
00:00:16.042 --> 00:00:25.888
Phileas Fogg was hoping they would reach San Francisco by December 2, New York by December 11, and London, England, by December 20.

3
00:00:25.889 --> 00:00:30.568
According to the wager, his trip was to conclude by December 21.

4
00:00:30.569 --> 00:00:36.119
Mr. Fogg's young female companion, Aouda, felt herself growing attached to her protector.

5
00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:39.762
Mr. Fogg's silent but generous nature impressed her.

6
00:00:39.763 --> 00:00:45.535
The more time she spent with him, the more she began to feel emotions stronger than simple gratitude.

7
00:00:45.536 --> 00:00:50.458
Her new feelings, however, did not seem to have any effect on Mr. Fogg.

8
00:00:50.459 --> 00:00:54.648
Passepartout did not fail to notice the state of the lady's heart.

9
00:00:54.649 --> 00:00:58.831
She was now as impatient as Fogg was over any delay in their journey.

10
00:00:58.832 --> 00:01:03.523
He assured her that the most difficult part of the journey had already passed.

11
00:01:03.524 --> 00:01:11.015
A railway train from San Francisco to New York and a steamer from New York to Liverpool would bring them to the end of their incredible travels.

12
00:01:11.016 --> 00:01:18.276
Now that Fix was out of their way, no obstacles could slow their progress. At least, that's what Passepartout thought.

13
00:01:18.277 --> 00:01:21.835
Detective Fix, however, was also on the General Grant.

14
00:01:21.836 --> 00:01:25.308
And he had the warrant for Mr. Fogg's arrest in his hand.

15
00:01:25.309 --> 00:01:29.216
It had been waiting for him at the English consulate in Yokohama.

16
00:01:29.217 --> 00:01:33.526
Unfortunately, now that Fogg had left English territory, it was useless.

17
00:01:33.527 --> 00:01:37.317
Fogg could not be arrested outside English territories.

18
00:01:37.318 --> 00:01:42.984
Six days out of Yokohama, Passepartout came face-to-face with Fix on the forward deck.

19
00:01:42.985 --> 00:01:50.427
Without a word Passepartout grabbed Fix by the throat and began to punch him, to the amusement of a group of Americans close by.

20
00:01:50.428 --> 00:01:58.303
When Passepartout had finished, Fix stood up and looked at his adversary coldly. "Are you done?" he asked.

21
00:01:58.304 --> 00:02:02.201
"For now . . . yes," said Passepartout with satisfaction.

22
00:02:02.202 --> 00:02:06.036
"Then let me have a word with you," said Fix.

23
00:02:06.037 --> 00:02:09.827
"You have given me a thrashing, and I expected it.

24
00:02:09.828 --> 00:02:16.217
Up to this time, I have been Mr. Fogg's adversary, but now I am on his side."

25
00:02:16.218 --> 00:02:21.769
"Aha!" cried Passepartout. "You are convinced he is an honest man!"

26
00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:26.437
"No," replied Fix. "I think he is a rascal!

27
00:02:26.438 --> 00:02:34.292
As long as Mr. Fogg was on English ground, it was my duty to detain him until the warrant for his arrest arrived.

28
00:02:34.293 --> 00:02:42.631
But now I want him back on English soil as quickly as possible, and I'll do what I can to help him reach it. Are we friends?"

29
00:02:42.632 --> 00:02:49.072
"Friends? No," replied Passepartout. "But allies perhaps."

30
00:02:49.073 --> 00:02:53.748
On December 2, the General Grant reached San Francisco.

31
00:02:53.749 --> 00:02:57.501
Mr. Fogg had neither gained nor lost a single day.

32
00:02:57.502 --> 00:03:04.039
It was seven o'clock in the morning when Mr. Fogg, Aouda, and Passepartout set foot on American soil.

33
00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:09.652
At the first news vendor he saw, Mr. Fogg inquired when the next train left for New York.

34
00:03:09.653 --> 00:03:15.977
The train was scheduled to leave at 6:00 p.m. so they had the entire day to spend in the California city.

35
00:03:15.978 --> 00:03:21.174
They hired a carriage and went to the International Hotel to dine.

36
00:03:21.175 --> 00:03:27.368
After breakfast Mr. Fogg and Aouda were about to leave for the passport office when Passepartout stopped them.

37
00:03:27.369 --> 00:03:33.251
"Perhaps I should purchase some rifles and revolvers while I am shopping, master?" asked Passepartout.

38
00:03:33.252 --> 00:03:37.509
He had heard stories of Indians attacking the trains.

39
00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:42.746
"I think it is a useless precaution, but do as you think best," replied Mr. Fogg.

40
00:03:42.747 --> 00:03:48.217
Mr. Fogg and Aouda had not walked two hundred steps, however, when they ran into Mr. Fix.

41
00:03:48.218 --> 00:03:52.139
The detective pretended to be wholly taken by surprise.

42
00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:59.690
Fix asked to accompany them on their walk around San Francisco, a request that Mr. Fogg readily granted.

43
00:03:59.691 --> 00:04:04.951
They soon found themselves on Montgomery Street, where a large crowd had collected.

44
00:04:04.952 --> 00:04:11.820
The crowd was marching down the street, cheering "Hooray for Camerfield!" and carrying large posters and banners.

45
00:04:11.821 --> 00:04:14.744
"It is a political meeting," said Mr. Fogg.

46
00:04:14.745 --> 00:04:19.370
"We had better not mingle in the crowd. There may be danger."

47
00:04:19.371 --> 00:04:24.931
"Yes," returned Fix. "A political rally can easily turn violent."

48
00:04:24.932 --> 00:04:29.952
They headed toward a flight of stairs at the side of the street that would take them out of the crowd.

49
00:04:29.953 --> 00:04:36.896
Just then another group, chanting "Hooray for Mandiboy!" came up the street from the opposite direction.

50
00:04:36.897 --> 00:04:43.471
The cheers quickly turned to shouts, and the posters turned to weapons as the opposing sides assaulted each other.

51
00:04:43.472 --> 00:04:48.646
It was too late to escape—Mr. Fogg, Aouda, and Fix were caught in the middle.

52
00:04:48.647 --> 00:04:54.963
Phileas Fogg and Fix bravely protected their fair companion, jostling roughly with the fighters.

53
00:04:54.964 --> 00:05:00.221
A tough, muscular fellow with a red beard rushed at Mr. Fogg with clenched fists.

54
00:05:00.222 --> 00:05:08.396
Before Mr. Fogg could clench his own fists, however, Fix stepped in and received the punch instead. Fix fell to the ground.

55
00:05:08.397 --> 00:05:16.745
Mr. Fogg stepped over him to face his opponent, but other combatants had moved between them and he couldn't reach the bearded man.

56
00:05:16.746 --> 00:05:20.551
"Yankee!" called Mr. Fogg. "We will meet again!"

57
00:05:20.552 --> 00:05:24.648
"Englishman!" retorted the other. "Do you challenge me?"

58
00:05:24.649 --> 00:05:29.521
"I do!" said Mr. Fogg confidently. "What is your name?"

59
00:05:29.522 --> 00:05:32.165
"Colonel Stamp Proctor," said the man.

60
00:05:32.166 --> 00:05:43.284
"Mine is Phileas Fogg, and I promise you we will face each other again!" Then the crowd swept them apart.

61
00:05:43.285 --> 00:05:47.746
It seems that Mr. Fogg makes friends wherever he goes . . . and enemies!

62
00:05:47.747 --> 00:05:51.641
I hope we don't meet Colonel Proctor again—or any Indians.

63
00:05:51.642 --> 00:05:55.046
Are you wondering why there are people called Indians in America?

64
00:05:55.047 --> 00:06:02.028
Well, let me explain. When the explorer Christopher Columbus first arrived in America, he thought he was in India.

65
00:06:02.029 --> 00:06:06.590
So when he encountered the Native Americans, he called them "Indians."

66
00:06:06.591 --> 00:06:12.755
Like Europeans and Asians, the group of people known as Native American consists of hundreds of different cultures.

67
00:06:12.756 --> 00:06:16.870
Two Native American tribes can differ as much as Asian people do.

68
00:06:16.871 --> 00:06:21.920
Native Americans have lived in North America long before Europeans invaded the continent.

69
00:06:21.921 --> 00:06:26.235
Since the arrival of Europeans, there has been a lot of fighting over land.

70
00:06:26.236 --> 00:06:33.797
So far we haven't seen any of these wars, but our journey isn't over yet!
